@page "/large-list"

<PageTitle>Large list · Bortable</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Large list</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        Three hundred rows in a scrolling panel. Drag a row toward the panel's edge and it scrolls - the browser
        scrolls for the mouse's native drag, Bortable for a finger, a pen, or the mouse with <code>ForceFallback</code>.
        Only a new drop position reaches .NET; <code>IsolateItems</code> keeps each step from rendering every row's
        template again.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-demo">
    <ScrollingPanel />
</div>

<DemoCode For="typeof(ScrollingPanel)" />

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Try it</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            Tick <code>ForceFallback</code>, drag <em>Row 1</em> to the bottom edge of the panel and hold it there: the
            panel scrolls, faster the closer the pointer is to the edge, and the row is carried along. Change
            <code>ScrollSensitivity</code> (how wide the edge band is) and <code>ScrollSpeed</code> (the most pixels per
            frame) and try again; untick <code>Scroll</code> and the panel stays put.
        </li>
        <li>Drag a row with the mouse and <code>ForceFallback</code> off: the browser scrolls the panel for its own drag.</li>
        <li>
            From the keyboard, pick <em>Row 1</em> up and hold <kbd>↓</kbd>: the row walks down, and the panel follows it
            into view at every step.
        </li>
        <li>
            Open the browser's console and run
            <code>(await import('/_content/Bit.Bortable/bit-bortable.js')).diagnostics()</code> after a drag: the
            positions sent to .NET (one per row crossed, not one per pointer move) and the average and slowest round trip.
            Tick <code>IsolateItems</code>, call <code>resetDiagnostics()</code>, and compare.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> What a drag costs</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>
            <strong>The pointer</strong> is followed and measured in the browser. A step that does not change the drop
            position - the pointer resting, or moving within a row - never reaches .NET. A step that does is one call; while
            it is on its way, the browser keeps the latest position for when it returns, so no position is lost and none
            queues up. That keeps a drag smooth on Blazor Server, too.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A new position</strong> changes the bound list in place and renders the list once, and the browser
            slides the rows when that render lands.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The render</strong> of a list renders all its items: a step through three hundred rows renders three
            hundred templates. <code>IsolateItems="true"</code> puts each template in a component of its own that a step
            skips - only the wrappers move. Leave it off when a template shows something other items' moves change, such
            as the item's position number: that would only catch up when the drag ends.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <div class="bb-note bb-note-warn">
        A list renders every item it is given: Blazor's <code>Virtualize</code> is not supported, because rows that are not
        rendered could be neither measured nor dropped beside. Page a list that is too long to render, or let a filter
        narrow it.
    </div>
</section>

<section class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Parameters on this page</h3>
    <div class="bb-scroll">
        <table class="bb-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>IsolateItems</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Renders each item's template in a component of its own, skipped on the steps of a drag.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>ForceFallback</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>The mouse drags with pointer events and a preview, as a finger does.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>Scroll</code></td><td><code>true</code></td><td>A finger, pen or <code>ForceFallback</code> drag near an edge scrolls the innermost element under the pointer that can still scroll that way, then the page.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>ScrollSensitivity</code></td><td><code>40</code></td><td>How close (px) to an edge the pointer must come to scroll it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>ScrollSpeed</code></td><td><code>16</code></td><td>The most pixels an edge scrolls per frame, at the very edge.</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="bb-table-key"><code>ItemLabel</code></td><td></td><td>How announcements name a row: <code>r =&gt; r.Name</code> rather than the record's <code>ToString()</code>.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>
